{
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "purpose": "Map the words Indian clinic assistants actually type onto the 377-term symptom vocabulary the model was trained on.",
  "why": [
    "The training vocabulary is US-English clinical terminology ('diarrhea', 'coryza', 'peripheral edema'). A rural clinic assistant in Uttar Pradesh types 'loose motion', 'zukam', 'pairon me sujan'.",
    "The matcher's safety rule is: no shared content word means no match. That rule is correct and must stay -- it is what stops 'body ache' reaching 'foreign body sensation in eye'. But it also means a synonym with zero overlapping tokens can never match, however obvious it is to a human.",
    "'loose motion' and 'diarrhea' share no token. Neither do 'diarrhoea' and 'diarrhea' -- one letter apart, but the token gate compares whole words. So the single most common presenting complaint in rural paediatrics was being silently dropped, and the model was scoring a dehydrated child on 'vomiting' alone.",
    "This layer is deterministic and reviewable: an alias resolves to exactly one canonical term, and every resolution is reported back to the caller so the assistant can see what the system understood."
  ],
  "rules": [
    "Every value MUST be an exact member of symptom_vocabulary.json['symptoms']. A typo here silently disables the alias -- the loader validates on startup and refuses to start with an unknown target.",
    "An alias may only mean ONE thing. Where a lay phrase is genuinely ambiguous ('stomach pain' -- upper? lower? burning?) it is deliberately ABSENT: the matcher reports it as unrecognised and the assistant is asked to be specific. Guessing a qualifier fabricates a clinical finding.",
    "Aliases are matched on whole word-windows, so 'loose motion' matches inside 'loose motion 5 times since morning' without matching the word 'loose' alone."
  ],
